
                    TERRAVISION (TM) - RELEASE NOTES

                         http://www.tvgeo.com/


OVERVIEW:
---------

TerraVision is a system for interactively browsing 3D representations
of large geographic areas. It can retrieve and merge massive volumes
of remotely located data, including aerial and satellite imagery,
topography, weather data, buildings, and other cultural features. The
data can be terabytes in size, distributed over multiple servers
across the Web.


HISTORY:
--------

TerraVision began life in 1993 as the primary application of the MAGIC
(Multidimensional Applications and Gigabit Internet Consortium)
project. TerraVision was the brain child of Dr. Yvan Leclerc at SRI
International, who kept its development going for almost a decade.
The funding for TerraVision completed in 2002 and as a result, SRI
decided to Open Source TerraVision so that the community can continue
to develop and utilize this unique system, and so that the legacy of
Yvan's work can continue to grow and evolve.

TerraVision is released under the Mozilla Public License 1.1 and the
full source code is hosted on the SorceForge website.


TO START UP TERRAVISION:
------------------------

1) Update your login script (e.g. .cshrc for csh) and add a TVHOME
   environment variable that points to the TerraVision directory, e.g.

     # csh script to setup environment for TerraVision
     setenv TVHOME /homedir/TerraVision
     set path = ($path $TVHOME/bin)

2) source your login script (e.g. "source .cshrc")

3) To run the Tcl/Tk TerraVision interface type, TerraVision

   (N.B. TerraVision is a shell script that runs the binary TclTV)


For help on using TerraVision, please refer to the User Guide at:

   http://www.tvgeo.com/user_guide/


VIEWING A SAMPLE DATASET:
-------------------------

There is a sample Database file included in the distribution as
TerraVision/etc/sample_database.xml. This contains a snapshot of all
terrain datasets available on the Internet2 TerraVision dataset
server. To try any of these, go to "File/Open Dataset/Open Terrain"
and select a GeoSet then click on "Open Dataset".

N.B. if the database file is out of date, you can update it with the
latest list of datasets by going to the "File/Open Terrain/Options"
button and clicking on "Re-read All". You can also download a number
of TerraVision datasets to your own machine so that they can be 
viewed locally without the need for a network connection. To download
datasets, go to:

   http://www.tvgeo.com/download_datasets.shtml   

Then extract the datasets to a directory named Pyramids, for example,
C:\TileSets\Pyramids. Then go to the "File/Servers" window and add
this directory as a new server.


